{"attack": true, "case": "The target misrepresents Popper by characterizing his methodology's preference as 'inductive' and as 'acknowledging its own lack of logical foundation,' then claiming 'Popper's system survives by admitting this gap as a pragmatic necessity.' Popper's critical rationalism was explicitly designed to provide a purely logical (deductive) solution to the problem of induction, via the asymmetry of falsification and verification. He openly embraced methodological conventions (rules of the game of science), but denied those conventions were inductive, pragmatic fudge-factors, or admissions of a logical gap. The target conflates Popper's methodological conventions—which he argued were logically motivated—with pragmatic conventionalism that concedes an inductive shortfall Popper himself rejected. The countercondition 'If Popper explicitly denied any conventional element in his methodology' is therefore malformed: Popper did NOT deny conventional elements, but he DID deny that those conventions were inductive or lacked logical foundation. Since that countercondition will never trigger (Popper embraced conventions), it fails to guard against the actual misattribution—namely, saddling Popper with an inductive 'gap' he explicitly denied existed.", "premise": "Popper explicitly held that his falsificationist methodology solves the problem of induction through purely deductive logic, not through pragmatic heuristics that concede an inductive gap. He embraced methodological conventions but denied they were inductive or pragmatically necessitated.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "3dcd2754c72fb3ce", "quote": "Accepts that logically, the preference is unjustified (inductive), but argues that human agency requires a working heuristic. Popper's system survives by admitting this gap as a pragmatic necessity rather than a logical feature."}], "counterexample": ["Popper, The Logic of Scientific Discovery: methodological rules are conventions, but they are motivated by the logical asymmetry between universal statements and singular observations—not by pragmatic necessity or acknowledged inductive gaps.", "Popper explicitly rejected Reichenbach-style pragmatic justifications of induction, arguing that the rationality of science rests on criticism and falsifiability, not on rules of thumb that concede logical deficit."], "successor_question": "If Popper's methodological conventions are logically motivated (by the verification/falsification asymmetry) rather than pragmatically necessitated, what account correctly describes the status of the preference for well-tested theories without misattributing an inductive gap to Popper?"}